  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nToday-Tonight... Tropical Depression Two (TD 2) remains located in \nthe northeast Gulf between two surface highs, one over the Atlantic \nand one of the western Gulf. TD 2 is forecast to slowly move north-\nnorthwest through the day, towards the Florida Panhandle and \nsouthern Alabama. Locally, southerly winds will persist with the \npressure gradient remaining tight across the area, with winds 10-15 \nmph with gusts 20-25 mph possible at times. While TD 2 will stay \nwell west of the local area, bands of gusty showers and lightning \nstorms will continue to move northward through east central Florida, \naided by the west and east coast sea breezes. There is a medium (40-\n60 percent) chance for showers and storms this afternoon, with the \ncoverage being widely scattered. Highest coverage is forecast across \nthe interior, where the sea breeze collision is forecast to occur. \nStorm motion will be 20-25 mph and generally towards to the \nnortheast. \n\nThe environment supports convection this afternoon with forecast \nsoundings showing ample instability (MUCAPE 2000-2500 J/kg), steep \nlow-level lapse rates, sufficient shear (SFC-6km shear of ~ 15 KT), \nand plenty of downdraft potential (DCAPE 700-900 J/kg). Main storm \nhazards will be frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds of 50-55 \nmph, and locally heavy rainfall of 1-3\". Some minor, localized, \nflooding will also be possible in some locations in training rain \nbands.  \n\nAny lingering shower and storm activity is forecast to dissipate or \nmove out of the area late evening, with mostly dry conditions \nforecast each night. However, can not rule out a few showers \nbrushing the coast as showers continue across the Atlantic waters \nthrough the overnight.  Near to slightly above normal temperatures \nwill continue with afternoon high temperatures in the low mid 90s \nand peak heat indices of 100-107 degrees. Overnight lows will \ncontinue to be in the low to mid 70s. \n\nTuesday-Thursday... Upper level ridging across the Plains and the \nDeep South will stay in place through the time period. At the \nsurface, high pressure across the Atlantic will strengthen and \ncontinue to build across the Florida peninsula and into the Gulf. \nThis will allow TD 2 to move westward along the northern Gulf Coast \nand towards Texas through late week, away from east central Florida. \nLocally, drier air will move into the area on Tuesday, with forecast \nPW values ranging from 1.5-1.7\", which will result in reduced rain \nchances through the period. Lower than normal rain chances are \nforecast, with a low (20-30 percent) chance of rain and lightning \nstorms each day. Southerly flow will persist through the period, \nwith the pressure gradient relaxing on Tuesday, resulting in winds \nslackening. The east coast sea breeze will form each afternoon, \nturning the winds onshore (east to southeast) behind the sea breeze. \nA slight warming trend through mid week, with afternoon highs in the \nmid 90s, even nearing the upper 90s at times. These temperatures \ncombined with high humidity will produce peak heat indices of 100-\n107F. A Heat Advisory may be needed, will continue to monitor.  \n\nFriday-Sunday...  Upper level high pressure over the Plains and the \nDeep South will remain in place as a trough over the Midwest shifts \neastward towards the Atlantic coast through Sunday. At the surface, \nthe Atlantic high pressure will shift southward through the weekend \nas a low pressure system pushes off the coast of the Carolinas. An \nassociated boundary will dig through the Deep South into late week \nand into northern Florida before stalling over the weekend. Locally, \nmoisture will increase over the area from the stalled boundary. \nHowever, there remains some uncertainty to how much moisture will \npush into Florida, and will depend on how far south the boundary \nmakes it and where it stalls. For now have a low (20-30 percent) \nchance for rain and storms on Friday and a low to medium (20-50 \npercent) chance on the weekend. The pressure gradient will remain \nrelaxed over the area through the weekend, with south to southeast \nwinds on Friday turning south to southwest over the weekend. The \neast coast sea breeze will form each day, backing the winds onshore \n(east to southeast) each afternoon. Hot temperatures will continue, \nwith afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s, with Saturday being the \nwarmest day of the weekend. Coupled with high humidity, peak heat \nindices will be in the above 100 F. Overnight lows will be in the \nmid to upper 70s.",
